>>   You see here a +0 elven dagger.
>>   Zap which spell? knock
>>   Which direction?
>>   You feel lucky!
>
>> Not that I'm suggesting that particular usage; it's way too obscure a
>> combination to be fair ...
>
> Too easy to become lucky, but not too obscure.

   I'm not sure we're using the same meaning of "obscure"; I wasn't nearly
clear enough in my original post. I meant, there's no reason for the
unspoiled player to suspect that standing on a wooden object and zapping
"knock" would do anything to his luck --- it's too much of a "reach,"
for me. And if he happened upon the effect by accident, he probably
wouldn't realize the connection between the zap, the object, and the
luck bonus.
   I'm not saying "knock on wood" is an obscure idiom (although Robert
Shaw points out that Britons may know it as "touch wood" instead); I'm
saying that that particular mechanism is just too weird and complicated
to be fair to unspoiled players.

   I have the same objection to many of the "read a [cursed] scroll of
[foo] while [confused|hallucinating]" effects, by the way. The effect
on the player should be "wow! TDTTOE!" (The Dev Team Thinks Of 
Everything), not "hmph, TDTCCUWAWTFTEIO" (The Dev Team Couldn't
Come Up With A Way To Fit That Effect In Otherwise).


> Besides, the player
> should/would get a (cryptic) clue for casting knock on a locked
> (presumably wooden) chest or door.  Those objects not giving luck
> outright, but rather pointing the (clever) player in the right
> direction, would be typically Nethackish.

   But that's the primary use of "knock"! You couldn't have each and
every "knock" spell giving cryptic clues about luck bonuses, when what
the player really wants to do is open a door --- that's just silly.
IMHO, the primary use of "knock" should remain unlocking doors and
chests, even for spoiled players.


